(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-male-gu-prostate-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Male GU (genitourinary) & Prostate Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Urinary incontinence, one of the screened history items, refers to...","choices":["complete inability to urinate at all","excessive thirst","involuntary loss of urine","blood in the urine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Urinary incontinence refers to the involuntary loss of urine, an important symptom to screen for as part of the lower urinary tract/GU (genitourinary) history. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"If a patient refuses the genital examination, the examiner's most appropriate next step is to...","choices":["perform the exam despite the refusal, since it is clearly medically indicated","end the entire clinical encounter immediately and reschedule for later","document the refusal briefly and move on without further discussion","discuss the patient's reasons for refusal respectfully, without forcing the exam"],"answer":3,"exp":"Respecting patient autonomy means discussing the reasons for refusal and addressing concerns where possible, without proceeding with the exam against the patient's wishes. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Palpating the epididymis as a distinct step from palpating the testis itself is important because the epididymis is normally...","choices":["located anterior to the testis and fused closely enough that it cannot be palpated separately","not a normal structure, but rather always a sign of chronic infection in healthy men","identical in location and consistency to the testis, requiring no separate assessment on exam","a separate, comma-shaped structure posterior to the testis, distinguishable on exam from the testis itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"The epididymis is a distinct, comma-shaped structure that sits posterior to the testis; palpating it separately allows the examiner to distinguish epididymal findings (often more likely benign/infectious, for example, epididymitis or a spermatocele) from testicular findings (which carry more concern for malignancy when nodular). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Acute epididymitis classically presents with...","choices":["a complete absence of any palpable abnormality despite significant active infection","a painless, firm nodule on the testis itself, developing gradually without tenderness","gradual onset of scrotal pain and tenderness localized to the epididymis, often with associated swelling","sudden onset of severe pain with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epididymitis typically presents with a more gradual onset of pain and tenderness localized to the epididymis (posterior to the testis), often with associated swelling and sometimes fever, in contrast to the sudden, severe pain of testicular torsion. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"During a testicular self-exam, a patient is taught to roll each testis gently between the thumb and fingers primarily to...","choices":["assess overall pulmonary function, in a manner loosely similar to spirometry testing","estimate blood pressure by mimicking the compression of a manual blood pressure cuff","feel for the smooth, firm consistency of a normal testis and detect any new lumps or changes","stimulate additional testosterone release from the underlying Leydig cells directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gently rolling the testis between thumb and fingers lets the patient become familiar with its normal smooth, firm consistency, making it easier to notice any new lumps, hardness, or changes in future self-exams. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Exposing only the areas being examined at any given time, whether the patient is standing or sitting, primarily serves to...","choices":["minimize unnecessary exposure and preserve patient dignity throughout the exam","make the exam take noticeably longer without any real clinical benefit","serve no real purpose beyond following an old-fashioned exam tradition","eliminate the separate need for gloves during genital palpation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Exposing only what is currently being examined, regardless of position, minimizes unnecessary exposure and helps preserve the patient's dignity and comfort throughout this sensitive exam. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Erectile dysfunction, as a screened history item, may have underlying causes spanning...","choices":["it is never a relevant question to ask during a routine GU history","vascular, neurologic, hormonal, psychological, or medication-related factors","only medication side effects, never any other physiologic cause","only psychological factors, with physical causes never contributing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Erectile dysfunction can stem from a broad range of underlying causes — vascular disease, neurologic conditions, hormonal imbalances, psychological factors, or medication side effects — making it an important, broad history question rather than pointing to one single cause. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Palpating the posterior surface of the prostate, one of the special techniques/maneuvers listed, is performed via which approach?","choices":["a transabdominal approach only, using deep palpation over the suprapubic region","by external palpation of the perineum only, using firm pressure with no internal component","by auscultation with a stethoscope placed directly over the perineal midline","a transrectal approach, using a gloved, lubricated finger (as in the DRE (digital rectal examination))"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior surface of the prostate is palpated transrectally, using a gloved, lubricated index finger inserted into the rectum, the same basic approach used in the digital rectal examination. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Cryptorchidism refers to...","choices":["an undescended testis, not palpable in the scrotal sac","a testis that is present but is abnormally firm and nodular","dilated veins above the testis, forming a 'bag of worms'","excessive fluid collecting around the testis itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cryptorchidism is the condition in which one or both testes fail to descend into the scrotum, so on exam the affected testis is not palpable in its expected scrotal location. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Comparing the left and right sides during the male GU (genitourinary) exam (for example, testis size, spermatic cord findings) is useful because...","choices":["asymmetry between sides can be a meaningful clue to underlying pathology","the two sides are never formally compared during a routine GU examination","symmetry is entirely irrelevant to interpreting any exam findings correctly","only the right side is ever routinely examined during a standard workup"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because paired structures are normally fairly symmetric, comparing left and right sides helps the examiner detect asymmetry (for example, one testis significantly larger, firmer, or more tender than the other), which can be an important clue pointing toward pathology. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A painless ulcer (chancre) noted on penile inspection should raise concern for...","choices":["a completely normal finding","primary syphilis","a hydrocele","benign prostatic hyperplasia"],"answer":1,"exp":"A painless genital ulcer (chancre) is the classic presentation of primary syphilis, an important consideration when penile lesions are noted on inspection. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Documenting the duration and progression of any GU (genitourinary) symptoms (acute versus chronic) helps the examiner primarily by...","choices":["having no bearing whatsoever on the assessment of clinical urgency","entirely replacing the need for any subsequent physical exam findings","applying only to cardiac complaints, never to genitourinary ones","helping distinguish urgent/emergent causes from more indolent, chronic processes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Symptom timeline is a key piece of information: sudden, severe symptoms (for example, acute scrotal pain) raise concern for emergencies like torsion, while gradually progressive symptoms suggest a more indolent process, guiding appropriate urgency of workup. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"If transillumination of a scrotal mass shows no light transmission (the mass appears opaque), this finding is more consistent with...","choices":["a simple hydrocele, which would typically transilluminate with a bright, glowing light","no further evaluation being needed, since opacity here is actually a reassuring sign","a solid mass, warranting further evaluation (for example, ultrasound) to rule out malignancy","a completely normal testis, with the opacity simply confirming healthy tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"A scrotal mass that does not transilluminate (remains opaque) suggests a solid, rather than fluid-filled, structure, which raises greater concern for malignancy and warrants further imaging evaluation such as ultrasound. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Maintaining a professional, matter-of-fact demeanor throughout the male GU (genitourinary) exam (rather than appearing rushed or uncomfortable) primarily helps to...","choices":["replace the separate, required step of obtaining informed consent","reduce patient embarrassment and build trust during a sensitive exam","have essentially no real effect on the patient's overall experience","speed up the exam mainly by skipping otherwise necessary steps"],"answer":1,"exp":"A calm, professional, matter-of-fact demeanor helps normalize the exam for the patient, reducing embarrassment and helping build the trust needed for the patient to remain cooperative and at ease. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Scrotal edema without a clear discrete mass, especially if bilateral, may suggest a more systemic process such as...","choices":["heart failure, kidney disease, or liver disease causing generalized fluid retention","always indicating a discrete, localized testicular tumor rather than a systemic process","a completely normal anatomic variant requiring no further clinical evaluation","testicular torsion, which classically presents as acute, unilateral, severe pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bilateral scrotal edema without a discrete mass can reflect systemic fluid-retaining conditions such as heart failure, kidney disease (for example, nephrotic syndrome), or liver disease (for example, cirrhosis with ascites), rather than a localized GU (genitourinary) process. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Good lighting during inspection of the penis and scrotum is important mainly because it...","choices":["allows the examiner to reliably visualize subtle skin lesions, discharge, or color changes","is only relevant to the prostate exam, which relies on palpation rather than vision","replaces the separate need for proper patient positioning during the inspection","is not necessary since the entire genital exam relies solely on palpation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Adequate lighting is essential for reliably visualizing often-subtle findings, such as small lesions, discharge, or color changes, during the inspection portion of the exam. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Asking about prior GU (genitourinary) surgeries (for example, vasectomy, hernia repair) as part of the history is useful because it...","choices":["has essentially no bearing on interpreting current exam findings, regardless of prior procedures","provides context for exam findings that might otherwise seem abnormal (for example, surgical scars, altered anatomy)","is relevant only to female patients undergoing a separate gynecologic surgical history intake","entirely replaces the separate need for performing any subsequent physical exam findings altogether"],"answer":1,"exp":"Knowing about prior GU (genitourinary) surgeries helps the examiner correctly interpret exam findings — such as scars, absent structures, or altered anatomy — that might otherwise be misinterpreted as new abnormalities. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A tense, tender, fluctuant scrotal swelling of rapid onset, distinct from the testis, may represent...","choices":["a scrotal abscess","a completely normal scrotal finding","Peyronie's disease","cryptorchidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"A tense, tender, fluctuant swelling of rapid onset in the scrotal wall (distinct from the testis itself) is suggestive of a scrotal abscess, a localized infectious collection requiring prompt evaluation. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"In the context of prostate cancer counseling, explaining that PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is not perfectly specific for cancer (other conditions can also raise it) is important so that patients understand...","choices":["PSA (prostate-specific antigen) testing is completely useless clinically and should never be offered to patients","an elevated PSA (prostate-specific antigen) warrants further evaluation but does not, by itself, confirm a cancer diagnosis","an elevated PSA (prostate-specific antigen) result always means prostate cancer is definitively present","PSA (prostate-specific antigen) testing has no false positives and is a perfectly specific cancer marker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because PSA (prostate-specific antigen) can be elevated by other conditions (such as prostatitis or BPH (benign prostatic hyperplasia)) in addition to cancer, patients should understand that an elevated result prompts further evaluation rather than being an automatic cancer diagnosis on its own. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Standard precautions during the male GU (genitourinary) exam (gloves, appropriate draping) exist primarily to protect against...","choices":["transmission of infection between patient and examiner via contact with genital skin/mucosa","nothing meaningful; these precautions are purely old-fashioned traditional formalities","improving the fine tactile accuracy of the examiner's palpation findings overall","preventing patient hypothermia only, with no infection-control purpose at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Standard precautions, including gloves, are designed to minimize the risk of infection transmission in either direction during any exam involving contact with mucous membranes or genital skin. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Assessing the consistency of the testis on palpation (should be smooth, homogeneous, and slightly firm) is important because a change in this baseline consistency is often...","choices":["never clinically significant, regardless of the degree of change","assessed only by visual inspection, never through palpation technique","only relevant in patients older than sixty years of age","the earliest detectable sign of an underlying mass or pathology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because a healthy testis has a fairly predictable smooth, homogeneous, slightly firm consistency, any focal change — a firmer area, an irregularity, or a nodule — can be the earliest detectable clue to an underlying mass or pathology, even before it's grossly visible. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A history of undescended testis in infancy/childhood is relevant to document in an adult patient because it is associated with an increased future risk of...","choices":["no increased risk of any type of cancer","testicular cancer, even after surgical correction","a decreased risk of future testicular cancer","increased risk of prostate cancer only, not testicular"],"answer":1,"exp":"A history of cryptorchidism (undescended testis), even if surgically corrected in childhood, is associated with an increased lifetime risk of testicular cancer, making this an important detail to document and factor into ongoing surveillance. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"An inguinal hernia that can be manually reduced (pushed back into the abdominal cavity) but recurs with straining, compared with one that cannot be reduced (incarcerated), differs in that an incarcerated hernia...","choices":["is trapped outside the abdominal cavity and carries risk of strangulation, a surgical emergency","is, in fact, easier to manually push back into the abdominal cavity than a reducible one","requires no urgent evaluation and can safely wait for a routine follow-up appointment","is always considered clinically less significant than a simple, reducible hernia is"],"answer":0,"exp":"An incarcerated hernia is trapped and cannot be pushed back (reduced) into the abdominal cavity; this raises concern for strangulation (compromised blood supply to the trapped tissue), a surgical emergency, in contrast to a simple reducible hernia. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"When performing the special technique of evaluating for an inguinal/femoral hernia with the patient standing, this position is used because it...","choices":["entirely replaces the separate need for palpation during the hernia assessment","increases intra-abdominal pressure via gravity, making a hernia more likely to become apparent","actually decreases the overall likelihood of detecting a true hernia bulge","is a position that is required only for female patients undergoing this exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Standing (often combined with Valsalva/coughing) increases intra-abdominal pressure via gravity, which makes a hernia more likely to bulge and become palpable/visible than it might be with the patient supine. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Explaining each step of the exam as you perform it (rather than silently examining) is a technique that primarily helps to...","choices":["is generally discouraged during this exam, per recommended standard technique","keep the patient informed and reduce anxiety about what's happening/being touched next","slow the exam down noticeably, providing no meaningful benefit to the patient","entirely replace the need for the initial explanation given before the exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Narrating each step as it happens helps the patient anticipate what will occur next, reducing anxiety and the potential for surprise or discomfort during a sensitive exam. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Penile lesions that are grouped, painful, and vesicular (small blister-like) most classically suggest...","choices":["Peyronie's disease","a normal finding requiring no evaluation","genital herpes","a hydrocele"],"answer":2,"exp":"Grouped, painful, vesicular lesions are the classic HSV (herpes simplex virus), distinct from the painless ulcer of syphilis or the cauliflower-like papules of condyloma. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A family history of prostate cancer in a first-degree relative is clinically relevant to gather because it...","choices":["increases the patient's own risk and may warrant earlier or more frequent screening discussion","matters only when discussing a female relative's own separate breast cancer risk","has essentially no bearing at all on the patient's own individual cancer risk","actually decreases the urgency of any future screening discussion needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"A first-degree relative with prostate cancer meaningfully increases a patient's own risk, which is factored into shared decision-making about when and how often to discuss/pursue screening. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"If a patient is examined while sitting rather than supine, appropriate exposure technique still requires...","choices":["no draping being needed at all whenever the patient happens to be seated","full exposure of the entire body, regardless of the patient's position","exposing only the genital/inguinal area currently being examined, regardless of position","an entirely different set of draping rules whenever the patient is sitting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Regardless of whether the patient is sitting or supine, the same principle applies: expose only the area currently being examined, preserving modesty and comfort in either position. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"After completing inspection and palpation of the genitalia, examining the inguinal lymph nodes is a reasonable additional step because enlarged nodes there could indicate...","choices":["only pulmonary pathology, since inguinal nodes are said to drain the lower lung fields","a local infectious or inflammatory process, or less commonly a malignancy, draining from the genital region","only cardiac pathology, since inguinal nodes are believed to drain directly from the heart","nothing clinically relevant, since inguinal nodes are rarely if ever normally palpable"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inguinal region is a drainage basin for lymphatics from the genitalia and lower extremities, so palpable inguinal lymphadenopathy can reflect a local infectious/inflammatory process (for example, an STI (sexually transmitted infection)) or, less commonly, malignancy, making it a reasonable extension of the exam. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A testis notably smaller and softer than its contralateral counterpart, without acute pain, may reflect...","choices":["testicular atrophy, which can result from prior trauma, infection, torsion, or other chronic processes","always indicates a current, acute episode of testicular torsion requiring emergency surgery","a completely normal anatomic finding that requires absolutely no further history-taking","a normal anatomic variant that is present equally in essentially all healthy men"],"answer":0,"exp":"A smaller, softer testis compared with the other side, particularly without acute pain, suggests testicular atrophy — a chronic change that can follow prior trauma, infection (for example, mumps orchitis), previous torsion, or other long-standing processes affecting that testis. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"}]};
